// Time And Space Complexity ->

// What is time complexity -> Time Complexity tells us how much time an algorithm takes as the input size
    increases . We dont measure it in seconds , but in terms of number of steps / operations .

    example ->      int sum = 0;
                    for(int i = 1;i<=n;i++){
                        sum+=i;
                    }

    The loop runs n times , so time complexity = O(n)

    Analogy = Think of solving math problems
            1. 10 problems takes some time
            2. 100 problems takes more time
            3. The time grows with the number of problems (input size n)

// What is space complexity -> Space complexity tells us how much memory an algorithm needs , it includes
    memory for :
        1. Variables
        2. Data Structures ( arrays , linked lists , etc )
        3. Function calls ( stack memory )

    example -> int arr[1000];  // it uses memory for 1000 integers

    This has space complexity O(n) if array size depends on input n .

    Analogy = Think of solving problems on paper
            1. If you use one page -> low space complexity
            2. If you use a big notebook -> High space complexity

// Time Vs Space trade-off -> Sometimes , we need to choose between saving time or saving space
    Example 1 : Calculate Fibonacci numbers ;
        Recursive Method -> less memory , but very slow ( repeat calculations )

    Dynamic programming (DP) -> uses extra memory (stores result) but is much better .

// Algorithms ->
    1. Algorithm Complexity Analysis -> when we design an algorithm , we want to know :
        How fast will it run ? -> time complexity
        How much memory will it need ? -> space complexity

        complexity analysis helps us compare algorithms without running them on a machine

    2. Time vs Space Trade Offs -> Sometimes , faster algorithms use more memory and less memory
        means slower speed .

        Examples :
         Storing all results in a lookup table (extra space) makes searching very fast .
         Not Storing results -> Saves memory , but increase consumption time .

        Real World Analogy :
         Think of studying for an exam :
         If you make notes (extra Space) -> you can revise quickly (less time)
         If you don't make notes -> you save pages , but need more time to revise .

    3. Asymptotic Notations -> Asymptotic Notations describe the growth rate of an algorithm as input
        size (n) becomes very large .

        we compare algorithms independent of machine or actual values , focusing only on how they scale .
        (a) Big-O    (O) -> upper bound

        worst-case growth rate

        The algorithm will not grow faster than this .
        Example : In linear search time is O(n)
            O(n) = algorithm takes at most n steps for input size n .

        (b)  Big - Omega (omega symbol) -> Lower bound

        Best - case growth rate .

        The algorithm will take at least this much time .
        Example : In linear Search ,
            best case = omega(1) , found in first step .

        Graph : algorithm will always stay above or on this curve .

        (c) Theta (ø) - Tight Bound

        Both upper and lower bound are the same .
        Gives the average . expected growth rate

        Example : In linear Search ,
            average case = Ø(n)

        Graph : Algorithm is sandwiched between upper and lower bounds .

